RUN: %PLC -c --fpic %S/retained_fat_pointer_lib_a.st -o %t.base.o
RUN: %PLC -c --fpic -i %S/retained_fat_pointer_lib_a.st %S/retained_fat_pointer_overload_lib_a.st -o %t.lib_a.o
RUN: %PLC -c --fpic -i %S/retained_fat_pointer_lib_a.st %S/retained_fat_pointer_overload_lib_b.st -o %t.lib_b.o
RUN: %COMPILE -i %S/retained_fat_pointer_lib_a.st -i %S/retained_fat_pointer_overload_lib_a.st -i %S/retained_fat_pointer_overload_lib_b.st %S/retained_fat_pointer_overload_main.st %t.base.o %t.lib_a.o %t.lib_b.o && %RUN | %CHECK %s

Two libraries each declare a generic function named bind, with different generic constraints, that
binds a concrete instance to a function block's REFERENCE TO interface input. A generic template
emits no code, but its retained fat pointer global is still defined by the library's object, so the
globals of the two templates must not share a name. retained_fat_pointer_lib_a.st provides the
interface and the consumer.

CHECK: main: done
